WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.120
In the first film, Frizzled and Dishevelled helped Wnt save beta β-catenin from

2
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:13.280
destruction. How can those same two proteins align every hair on a fly wing without

3
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.260
beta β-catenin? They run a separate polarity system.

4
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:20.773
Look closely at this sheet of fly wing cells.

5
00:00:22.213 --> 00:00:26.433
Every cell grows one emerald hair, and every hair points toward the wing tip.

6
00:00:27.713 --> 00:00:31.813
This shared direction across the plane of a sheet is planar cell polarity.

7
00:00:33.153 --> 00:00:35.313
Yet no single cell can see the whole wing.

8
00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:41.787
To test how cells learn direction, Charles Vinson and Paul Adler made small patches

9
00:00:41.787 --> 00:00:44.287
lacking Frizzled inside normal wings.

10
00:00:45.647 --> 00:00:48.787
Then they looked closely at the normal cells beside each patch.

11
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.810
Normal hairs next to the patch point toward it.

12
00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.970
On the tip side, that means pointing backward, the wrong way.

13
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.210
On the body side, toward the patch is still toward the tip.

14
00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:07.110
Because genetically normal cells changed direction, cells must pass polarity

15
00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.030
information from neighbour to neighbour across the sheet.

16
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:15.993
Zoom into normal wing cells to see how each cell tells its two sides apart.

17
00:01:17.393 --> 00:01:22.333
Teal Frizzled and amber Dishevelled gather along the tip-side edge of every cell.

18
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:28.333
On the opposite edge, magenta Van Gogh gathers with its lavender partner, Prickle.

19
00:01:29.613 --> 00:01:31.433
Each cell builds two distinct sides.

20
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:32.433
Zoom into normal wing cells to see how each cell tells its two sides apart.

21
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:38.307
Across every shared border, silver Flamingo bridges one cell's Frizzled edge to its

22
00:01:38.307 --> 00:01:39.707
neighbour's Van Gogh edge.

23
00:01:40.947 --> 00:01:45.447
Each cell grows its actin hair right at its Frizzled edge, so every hair points

24
00:01:45.447 --> 00:01:46.447
together.

25
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.870
Notice what is absent from this entire picture.

26
00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:55.790
Planar polarity in the wing uses no beta β-catenin and no TCF.

27
00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:02.310
Instead of switching on TCF genes, Frizzled and Dishevelled set the place where each

28
00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.410
cell builds its hair.

29
00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:08.770
Vertebrates reuse these same polarity genes to shape early tissues.

30
00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:14.750
The future brain and spinal cord begin as a flat sheet called the neural plate.

31
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:21.390
Guided by polarity proteins and Wnt signals, cells identify which edge faces the

32
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:26.730
midline. They squeeze between neighbours to narrow the plate and fold it into a

33
00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:27.730
tube.

34
00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:29.403
Pause and predict.

35
00:02:31.103 --> 00:02:34.243
Suppose these cells could not tell which edge faces the midline.

36
00:02:35.523 --> 00:02:38.623
What would happen to the plate's width, and could its edges still meet?

37
00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:49.667
When planar polarity breaks, cells lose their bearings, and the neural plate can

38
00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:50.907
stay too wide to close.

39
00:02:52.387 --> 00:02:57.667
Disrupting these genes causes neural tube closure defects, including spina bifida.

40
00:02:59.027 --> 00:03:03.847
Whether aligning wing hairs or closing a tube, neighbours sort Frizzled to one edge

41
00:03:03.847 --> 00:03:04.807
so the sheet agrees.
